{
  "key": "mariner_3",
  "name": "Mariner 3",
  "rocket": "Atlas-Agena D",
  "payload": "Mars flyby spacecraft (Mariner C, 260.8 kg)",
  "status": "Failure (shroud did not separate)",
  "significance": "Mariner 3 was the first of NASA's two 1964 Mars probes. The protective shroud over the spacecraft failed to come off during launch, so its solar panels never opened and its battery ran down within about nine hours. The extra weight also left it short of the speed it needed for Mars, and it went into an orbit around the Sun instead. Engineers traced the fault to the shroud's fiberglass layers coming apart, built an all-metal shroud, and launched its twin, Mariner 4, three weeks later.",
  "statusNote": "Launched 1964-11-05 19:22:05 UT on an Atlas-Agena D from Cape Kennedy (Siddiqi, Beyond Earth; NASA NSSDCA). The payload shroud did not separate; battery power dropped to zero at T+8 h 43 min and the solar panels apparently never deployed. It ended in a heliocentric orbit of 0.983 x 1.311 AU (Siddiqi; NASA). Path calculated (estimate, drawn dashed): no trajectory data survives, only published orbit figures. Built with tools/build_solar_orbit.py: an orbit of the published size, starting at Earth's position at launch (JPL Horizons) and propagated two-body. Its size and shape follow the published figures; its orientation is an assumption (the launch point is taken as the ascending node). The orbit's inclination isn't published, so it is drawn in the plane of Earth's orbit; one full orbit (about 449 days) is shown.",
